WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:03.202
Gentlemen, welcome to the Forge.

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:04.770
[Wil] On your anvils
in front of you

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.405
is a bar of high carbon steel,

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.474
from which you will forge
a signature blade.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.611
However, as you may
have guessed, there's a twist.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.814
You will also have to forge
your blades using these.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.117
[cloth rustles]

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:21.521
These are all
random pieces of steel

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:22.488
from the urban environment.

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:26.492
We've got fire hydrants,
fire escape ladders,

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:30.663
subway grates,
road signs, pieces of railroad.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:33.900
Each item can only be selected
by one contestant.

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:35.101
So choose wisely.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.102
And in order
to keep things fair,

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:38.237
you will all be given

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:41.240
equal amounts
of the steel you choose.

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.176
Names were drawn from a hat.

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:45.811
Mareko, choose your item.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:48.080
I'm gonna go with the sign.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.982
David, go ahead and choose.

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.684
I choose the grate.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.186
Josh, you're up.
Choose your item.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:56.456
None of it's anything I
would ever use for a knife.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.858
[Josh]<i> So I'm feeling
 pretty nervous.</i>

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.160
I choose the stairs.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.428
[Wil] The blade parameters
are as follows.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.864
<i> The length of the blade,
 not including the tang,</i>

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.399
<i> must fall between 12
 and 14 inches.</i>

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:09.101
<i> The overall length
 of your weapon</i>

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:11.204
<i> must not exceed 22 inches.</i>

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:12.571
Feeling a little anxious.

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:14.607
[David]<i> This challenge
 is gonna be tough.</i>

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:18.211
<i> I decided I'm gonna make
 a long slender blade</i>

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:22.482 line:0%
<i> that I really liken
 to a bush knife.</i>

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.284
<i>What I'm gonna do is first take
 the high carbon steel</i>

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:26.652
and make it into a bar.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.855
<i> And then take two layers
 on either side of the grate</i>

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:31.424
<i> and laminate it together.</i>

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:33.726
[J] He's gotta be careful
blending those layers.

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:36.562
So he's got the high
carbon steel on the edge.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.964
[David]<i> This knife
 is the first knife I've made</i>

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:40.099
<i> since I crashed.</i>

00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:42.835
I'm excited. I'm back.
Let's do this.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.204
[Mareko]<i> I'm gonna make
 a chef's knife.</i>

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:48.440 line:0%
<i> Kitchen knives typically have
 a very thin edge.</i>

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.377 line:0%
<i> So my plan was just
 to forge the knife</i>

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.346
in a way, so I had
a more robust edge,

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.749
<i> which can withstand the impact
 of hitting a hard antler.</i>

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.051
I'm gonna forge
my high carbon bar

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:00.854
down into a flat stalk,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.421
<i> cut it up into a bunch
 of pieces,</i>

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:06.425
<i> and then I'm gonna mix that in
 with the street sign material</i>

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.127
<i> but only have
 the street sign material</i>

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:09.628
<i> on the outer edges.</i>

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:10.830
[fire roars]

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.065
[rock music plays]

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:14.767
[Josh]<i> I'd like
 to make a fighter.</i>

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.603 line:0%
<i> I don't generally make them
 12 inches long, though.</i>

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.840 line:0%
It starts to limit my design
capabilities at that length.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:22.474
I wonder what's up his sleeve.

00:02:22.574 --> 00:02:23.909
[Jason] His last name
is Smith, man.

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:24.978
He knows what he's doing.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.380
[Josh]<i> My game plan
 with that stair material</i>

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:29.115
<i>is to draw it out, thin it out,</i>

00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:31.184
<i> and get it on the outside
 of my billet.</i>

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.718 line:0%
<i> If that billet's a sandwich,</i>

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.554 line:0%
<i> it's got everything
 in the fridge, the pantry,</i>

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.455
and something
from the garage in it.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:38.457
[Wil] It looks like
Dave's dealing

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:39.559
with a little bit of a warp

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.061
or a bend to one direction
or another.

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.297
[David]<i> It's important
 to straighten the blade</i>

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.798
before you harden it.

00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:47.833
[J] Dave, what are you doing?

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:49.101
Dave!

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:50.937
Ah, he's gonna break it.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.806
[David]<i> If I bend it too much,
 it could crack.</i>

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.310
If the blade cracks,
I'm finished.

00:02:57.743 --> 00:03:01.080
<i> So I decided I'm gonna take
 the blade to the grinder.</i>

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.783
<i> And hopefully,
 it will work itself out.</i>

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:05.818
[clinking]

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:08.921
Looks like Mareko's preparation
is paying off.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:13.125
He's taking that blade
and suddenly, it's a knife.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.061
[Mareko] I got the blade
forged out really fast.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.964
I'm feeling pretty good
about the profile.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.201
<i> Now I could quench the blade.</i>

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:25.304
[Wil] Mareko's in.

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:27.373
<i> The first thing I wanna do
 is check it with a file</i>

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.509
because I know that's something
they're gonna do.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:31.711
[swishes]

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:35.715
And it's soft.

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.052
I can't submit a soft blade
to the judges.

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:41.487
<i> I have to heat-treat it
 a second time.</i>

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:45.624
<i> But I'm a little worried
 about running out of time.</i>

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:49.329
[Wil] There we go.
David is in the quench.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.763
And Josh has quenched.

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.532
[Jason] Yeah.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.368
[roars]

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:58.304
Not the hair on my arm.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.772
I'd like to say
that's the first time

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:03.042
I've burned the hair off my arm,
but it's happened many times.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.612
[Wil] Three, two, one!

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:09.749
Bladesmiths, drop your tools!
Shut down your machines!

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.885
[J] Bladesmiths, welcome
to the Strength Test.

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:14.386
We're gonna have some fun today.

00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:18.757
We're gonna take your knives
and strike antler ten times.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:21.194
-Oh, my [bleep].
-[J] Not only is this gonna test

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.895
the strength of the edge
of your knife,

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:26.132
but also the overall durability
of the knife itself.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.766
Josh, you're up first.
You ready?

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:28.501
I'm ready.

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:30.470
-I'm ready too.
-All right.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:35.574 line:0%
[Josh]<i> The Antler Test
 is something I do at home.</i>

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:37.910 line:0%
<i> And it's extremely aggressive
 on the edge.</i>

00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:39.946 line:0%
So I'm definitely a bit nervous.

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.385
[knife whacking]

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:56.128
-[giggles]
-Damn!

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:58.364
[serious music]

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:00.866
Well, Josh, we kinda have

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.968
a muscle of serration
in the middle now.

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:05.304
It felt good swinging.

00:05:05.737 --> 00:05:08.174
-[J] But that edge damage...
-[Josh] Mm-hmm.

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.508
-...is concerning.
-[Josh] Yeah.

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.910
[Josh]<i> There's a lot of edge
 deformation in my blade.</i>

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.315
I'm pretty much figured I'm
finished in this competition.

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.683
[J] Mareko, you ready?

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:18.984
I'm so excited.

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:21.653
[all laughing]

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.956
You know what, honestly,
that's two of us.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:25.857
[chuckling]

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.593 line:0%
[serious music]

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.835
[suspenseful music]

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:37.837
[knife whacking]

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:48.147
Looks a lot better than mine.

00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:50.516
[David] It's good, man.
It didn't break.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.852
Well, Mareko,
for the size of this knife,

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:57.490
it's one of the lightest knives
I've ever swung.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:58.958
You got deformation.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.227
-Got a couple little pieces out.
-[Mareko] Yeah.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.362
But it bit
into the antler really well.

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:06.165
-[Mareko] Cool.
-And it held together.

00:06:06.231 --> 00:06:07.867
-[J] Good job.
-Thanks.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:09.568
[J] David, you're up next.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.303
-You ready?
-I'm ready.

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:13.706 line:0%
[serious music]

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.149
[rock music plays]

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.021
[knife whacking]

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:36.629
[serious music]

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:39.732
Nice job.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.269
David, you have a significant
bend in this blade

00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:44.837
after the testing.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.007
[J] We got some chipping,
some damage.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.542
Right in that center spot there.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:54.680
-But it survived. Good job.
-All right. Thanks.

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:58.417
[Jason] We're gonna test
the sharpness of your blades

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:01.053
by maybe cutting
through all these apples.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.955
It will depend on your knife.

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:04.723
If it's sharp enough,

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.293
we should have a clean cut
all the way through.

00:07:07.793 --> 00:07:09.896
-Josh, are you ready?
-[Josh] I'm ready.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.701
[suspenseful music]

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:17.703
[swishes]

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:25.911
Oh, nice. All right, brother.
Good job.

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:27.346
Thanks.
[sniffles]

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:29.582
Well, Josh, despite your edge

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:32.918
having chipping and wrinkling
from the antler damage,

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:37.089
it's still sharp enough
to cut through the apples.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.757
-[Jason] Well done.
-Thank you.

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:40.525
[Jason] Mareko, you ready?

00:07:40.592 --> 00:07:41.927
-Yeah, man.
-All right.

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:43.663
[suspenseful music]

00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:47.834
[swishes]

00:07:50.469 --> 00:07:52.438
[David] Nice job. Nice job.

00:07:52.504 --> 00:07:54.807
-Nice work.
-All right, man.

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:58.177
Mareko, it went through
like a lightsaber, man.

00:07:58.243 --> 00:07:59.378
[imitating lightsaber whoosh]

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:00.780
It just zipped right through

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:01.880
like they weren't even there.

00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:03.516
-[Mareko] Thanks.
-Well done.

00:08:03.582 --> 00:08:05.318
-Thank you.
-[Jason] David.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:07.252
-You ready?
<i> -Hai.</i>

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:08.888
All right, let's do it.

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:12.724
[David]<i> My knife is definitely
 more of an apple slicer.</i>

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:16.262
Jason is going to cut
through the apples, I'm sure.

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:18.965
[suspenseful music]

00:08:20.332 --> 00:08:21.701
[swishes]

00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:26.138
[tense music]

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:31.377
David, during
the Antler Testing, it has bent.

00:08:31.443 --> 00:08:33.112
[Jason] The bend
is actually curving up.

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:36.915
And if I'm lying down like this,
it's accentuated.

00:08:37.482 --> 00:08:39.151
So as I'm trying to travel down,

00:08:39.618 --> 00:08:40.886
it's very difficult

00:08:40.952 --> 00:08:43.922
to accurately slice
through those apples.

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.693
Well, this is the Olympics
of knife-making, right?

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:49.528
I'll take a bronze any day.

00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:51.764
-[Jason] That's right.
-[laughter]

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:55.534
For that, David, I commend you
and I respect you very much.

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:56.802
Thank you very much.

00:08:57.269 --> 00:08:58.871
David, as you acknowledged,

00:08:58.971 --> 00:09:01.774
this time, the judges' decision
is an easy one.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.075
And I have to ask you
to please the Forge.

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:05.711
Thank you, guys.

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:08.014
I'm feeling a little sad.

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:09.581
[David]
<i> Although I'm not ashamed.</i>

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:13.418
I proved to myself
that I could make a knife again.

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:18.557
I know that people have had
much more difficult times

00:09:18.657 --> 00:09:19.992
in their life than I have.

00:09:20.092 --> 00:09:22.128
<i> But I would tell anyone</i>

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:24.897
<i> who had a setback in life</i>

00:09:24.963 --> 00:09:29.168
to get up, shake it off,
and start again.

